  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nA line of rain showers continues to traipse across the Northland \nthis afternoon, bringing light to moderate steady rainfall to much \nof the area. This line of showers is connected to a much larger \nsynoptic system dragging moisture from the Desert Southwest to Lake \nSuperior, but we are in the weakest part of this disturbance and \nexpect the rain to diminish as it moves east through this evening. \nExpect additional accumulations of a couple hundredths to a couple \ntenths. \n\nOnce today's rain moves out, a broad upper level trough settles over \nthe northern portion of the CONUS, blocking the Northland from much \nof the weather going on to our south. This will come along with \nlower dewpoints and fall like temperatures for the latter half of \nthe week. Before that, Wednesday will be on the warm side with highs \nreaching into the mid to high 70s, but then highs in the 50s and 60s \nreturn Thursday and into the weekend. As we end up on the receiving \nend of CAA with northwesterly flow, we'll see the potential for \nfrost/freeze concerns return. The first main risk looks to arrive \nThursday night into Friday morning, with nightly frost chances \ncontinuing through early next week. Another widespread freeze could \nbe possible Sunday night into Monday. \n\nA weak shortwave on the backside of high pressure sweeps across the \narea Saturday and could bring some scattered light showers. Not \nexpecting much out of this system, but some areas could see 0.25-\n0.5\" if some more robust showers move over the same area multiple \ntimes.",
